# Managed by homelab-postfix -- do not hand-edit, edit debconf answers
# instead (dpkg-reconfigure homelab-postfix). Postfix's own pgsql map
# format (see `man 5 pgsql_table`). Backs virtual_alias_maps: inbound
# routing only, so this query intentionally checks `active` alone --
# it must never depend on send_enabled (see
# domain-admin/README.md's "Multi-domain send-as" section for why
# receiving and sending are independently toggleable). Postfix's own
# map-lookup fallback (exact address, then bare @domain) does catch-all
# matching automatically -- source_pattern rows are stored exactly as
# Postfix expects ('@forge.name' or 'sales@forge.name'), no app-side
# wildcard logic needed. See ../../domain-admin/README.md and this
# package's own README.md "Multi-domain send-as" section.
#
# A REAL MAILBOX MUST ALWAYS BEAT THE CATCH-ALL. That is what the second
# arm of the UNION is for, and it is not optional tidiness -- without it
# the catch-all silently steals mail from every mailbox at the domain.
#
# Why it happens: Postfix consults virtual_alias_maps BEFORE
# virtual_mailbox_maps, and its own fallback turns a miss on
# 'user@domain' into a lookup of '@domain'. So a '@domain' catch-all row
# rewrites EVERY address at that domain -- including addresses that have
# their own mailbox, which never gets consulted. Found live 2026-10-27:
# a brand-new dmarc-reports@mailmasker.org mailbox had its first probe
# logged as `to=<permittivity@mailmasker.org>
# orig_to=<dmarc-reports@mailmasker.org>`, and johnboy@mailmasker.org --
# an existing, active account -- had been quietly diverted the same way.
# RCPT TO answers 250 either way, which is why it goes unnoticed.
#
# The previous remedy was a per-mailbox IDENTITY alias row (X -> X).
# That works but is bookkeeping nobody remembers: it is exactly what was
# missing for johnboy. This query makes it structural instead --
# "<username>@<domain> always goes to <username>" holds for every
# mailbox, with no row to forget.
#
# The arms, in order:
#   1. an EXPLICIT alias wins, so deliberate forwards still work;
#   2. otherwise, if the address IS an active mailbox, map it to itself,
#      so Postfix stops before reaching the '@domain' catch-all;
#   3. otherwise return nothing, and the catch-all applies -- which is
#      what a catch-all is actually for.
# The NOT EXISTS in arm 2 keeps arms 1 and 2 mutually exclusive: two
# rows would make Postfix deliver to both destinations.
#
# NOTE this needs SELECT on api.users(email, active). The runtime role
# already has it at COLUMN level for virtual_mailbox_maps, which is why
# `has_table_privilege(role,'api.users','SELECT')` reports false while
# the lookup works -- don't "fix" that by widening the grant.
hosts = __DB_HOST__:__DB_PORT__
user = __DB_USER__
password = __DB_PASS__
dbname = __DB_NAME__
query = SELECT destination FROM domainadmin.mail_aliases WHERE source_pattern='%s' AND active = true UNION ALL SELECT u.email FROM api.users u WHERE u.email='%s' AND u.active = true AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM domainadmin.mail_aliases a WHERE a.source_pattern='%s' AND a.active = true)
